\caption(The two actual singleton states produce a pair state with tids {0,1}. The same intersection is attempted but pruned at threshold three.)
\alttext(Two native boxes for bread and milk show their three tids, with arrows into the actual bread-milk intersection containing tids zero and one.)

> Derive the vertical search

Let D be the sequence of N baskets and U the number of distinct observed
items. For an itemset X, define T(X)={i : X is a subset of D_i}. Item support is |T(X)|, an absolute
row count rather than a proportion. Repeated baskets are distinct rows;
repeated item ids within one basket contribute only once. Empty baskets
retain their row indices. No empty itemset is returned.

For any prefix P and distinct extensions a<b,

\[
T(P\cup\{a,b\})=T(P\cup\{a\})\cap T(P\cup\{b\}).
\]

A row belongs to the intersection exactly when it contains both operand
sets, hence their union. Sorted tidsets allow a merge: advance the
smaller head, or emit an equal head and advance both. Each paired-head
step advances at least one cursor, giving O(|L|+|R|) work for an
intersection. The implementation counts those loop steps, not individual
machine comparisons or elapsed time.

Downward closure gives |T(X union {a})|<=|T(X)|. If X is infrequent,
every extension is infrequent and can be pruned. Starting with observed
singletons in ascending item-id order, emit a frequent prefix, form its
conditional extensions against later siblings, and descend. Increasing
ids give each nonempty subset a unique path. Induction over that path
establishes completeness; the intersection identity establishes support
correctness. At threshold zero, pruning disappears and every nonempty
subset of the observed universe is returned, even with zero support.

The core uses explicit DFS frames. It forms all sibling intersections
before descending, preserving the original recursive schedule. Trace
state creation order therefore differs from its emitted-state order.
The ordinary API returns canonical size/lexicographic order; it retains
no optional intersection history. Both calls use the same kernel.

> Preserve the original grocery example

The original eight baskets contain bread, milk, butter, beer and diapers.
The vertical-layout API now owns the normalization and row-index work
that previously lived in this report. The table lists every result at
minimum support two; the figure shows singleton supports. Apriori and
FP-growth are independent positive-threshold cross-checks here.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/eclat_groceries.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::vector<std::vector<std::size_t>> baskets{{0,1,2},{0,1},{1,2},{0,1,2},{3,4},{0,3,4},{3,4},{0,1,2,3}};
  const std::vector<std::string> labels{"bread","milk","butter","beer","diapers"};
  const auto vertical=dm::algorithms::vertical_tidsets(baskets);
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::eclat_trace(baskets,2);
  std::vector<double> counts;
  std::print("| singleton | original transaction ids | support |\n| :--- | :--- | ---: |\n");
  for(const auto& root:vertical){std::print("| {} |",labels.at(root.item));for(auto id:root.tids)std::print(" {}",id);std::print(" | {} |\n",root.tids.size());counts.push_back(root.tids.size());}
  std::print("\n| frequent itemset | support |\n| :--- | ---: |\n");
  for(const auto& f:t.itemsets)std::print("| {} | {} |\n",dm::algorithms::itemset_label(f.items,labels),f.support);
  const auto as_map=[](const auto& sets){std::map<std::vector<std::size_t>,std::size_t> out;for(const auto& f:sets)out.emplace(f.items,f.support);return out;};
  const bool apriori=as_map(t.itemsets)==as_map(dm::algorithms::apriori_frequent_itemsets(baskets,2));
  const bool fp=as_map(t.itemsets)==as_map(dm::algorithms::fp_growth_frequent_itemsets(baskets,2));
  if(!apriori||!fp)throw std::runtime_error("miner disagreement");
  std::print("\nOriginal groceries: {} sets; Apriori agrees {}; FP-growth agrees {}; {} actual intersections; {} matched tids; {} captured entries.\n",t.itemsets.size(),apriori,fp,t.work.intersections,t.work.matched_tids,t.captured_entries);
  for(std::size_t id=0;id<t.states.size();++id){const auto& s=t.states[id];
    if(s.itemset.items==std::vector<std::size_t>{0,1,2}||s.itemset.items==std::vector<std::size_t>{3,4}){
      std::print("State {}: {} intersect {} gives {}, support {}; tids",id,dm::algorithms::itemset_label(t.states.at(s.left).itemset.items,labels),dm::algorithms::itemset_label(t.states.at(s.right).itemset.items,labels),dm::algorithms::itemset_label(s.itemset.items,labels),s.itemset.support);
      for(auto tid:s.tids)std::print(" {}",tid);std::print(".\n");}}
  std::print("DFS state visits:");for(auto id:t.emitted)std::print(" {}",id);std::print(".\n");
  auto p=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();p.barplot(counts,labels,"",{37,99,235}).title("Original grocery singleton supports").x_label("Item").y_label("Transactions containing item").y_limits(0,6).size(900,500).save_svg("build/eclat_groceries.svg");
}
