\caption(The canonical embedded table needs no download or external data preparation. Counts describe these recorded passengers, with every frequent combination listed above.)
\alttext(Two vertically stacked native bar plots show seven Titanic attribute supports and the numbers of frequent one-, two- and three-item sets.)

These supports describe the observed table. Sex, class and survival
co-occurrences are neither causal effects nor a validated prediction
model. The support floor removes rare combinations; it does not remove
confounding, selection effects or the need for independent validation.
The three positive-threshold miners agree on this finite dataset.

> Boundary semantics and retained-history limits

At zero threshold, {0} and {1} in separate baskets produce the extra
itemset {0,1} with support zero. Eclat and Apriori currently agree on
that observed-universe convention; FP-growth currently returns only the
two positive-support singletons. Claims that all three implementations
always agree therefore need the positive-threshold qualification.
Thresholds above the row count yield no frequent sets. Arbitrary size_t
ids, including the maximum value, stay sparse identifiers.

The trace cap counts retained logical entries:

\[
C=N+\text{raw occurrences}
 +\sum_{s\in states}(1+|s.items|+|s.tids|)+|emitted|.
\]

The raw base is checked before input copying or normalization. Each state
is checked before copying its item/tid vectors into history; a working
intersection must already exist to determine its length. The cap excludes
canonical result copies, normalization and working frames, scalar fields,
string/allocator overhead and capacity. It is not a peak-memory bound.
The default is one million entries; zero disables only the optional
quota. Arithmetic checks remain, and failure exposes no partial trace.
The ordinary and work-only APIs have no optional capture quota.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/eclat_boundaries.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::vector<std::vector<std::size_t>> tiny{{0,1},{0,1},{0},{1}};
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::eclat_trace(tiny,2,28),unlimited=dm::algorithms::eclat_trace(tiny,2,0);
  int rejected=0;for(std::size_t cap:{9,27})try{(void)dm::algorithms::eclat_trace(tiny,2,cap);}catch(const std::length_error&){++rejected;}
  std::size_t roots=0,children=0;for(std::size_t i=0;i<t.states.size();++i){const auto cost=1+t.states[i].itemset.items.size()+t.states[i].tids.size();(i<t.singleton_count?roots:children)+=cost;}
  std::print("Exact cap: {} entries; disabled cap: {} entries; base/one-short rejections {}/2.\n",t.captured_entries,unlimited.captured_entries,rejected);
  std::print("Capture components: raw base {}; root states {}; intersection states {}; emitted ids {}.\n",dm::algorithms::eclat_trace_base_entries(tiny),roots,children,t.emitted.size());
  const auto zero=dm::algorithms::eclat_frequent_itemsets({{0},{1}},0);
  std::print("Zero threshold on disjoint singletons: Eclat {}; Apriori {}; FP-growth {}; pair support {}.\n",zero.size(),dm::algorithms::apriori_frequent_itemsets({{0},{1}},0).size(),dm::algorithms::fp_growth_frequent_itemsets({{0},{1}},0).size(),zero.back().support);
  const auto big=std::numeric_limits<std::size_t>::max();
  const auto sparse=dm::algorithms::eclat_trace({{big,big},{},{0},{big}},1);
  std::print("Sparse ids: {} rows; {} raw occurrences; {} incidences; {} frequent sets; maximum-id support {}.\n",sparse.work.transactions,sparse.work.raw_items,sparse.work.incidences,sparse.itemsets.size(),sparse.itemsets.at(1).support);
  std::print("Empty input sets {}; empty-basket input sets {}; threshold above N sets {}.\n",dm::algorithms::eclat_frequent_itemsets({},0).size(),dm::algorithms::eclat_frequent_itemsets({{},{}},0).size(),dm::algorithms::eclat_frequent_itemsets(tiny,5).size());
  auto p=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();p.barplot(std::vector<double>{double(dm::algorithms::eclat_trace_base_entries(tiny)),double(roots),double(children),double(t.emitted.size())},std::vector<std::string>{"Raw input","Root states","Pair state","Emitted ids"},"",{124,58,237}).title("Tiny trace: 28 retained logical entries").x_label("Counted component").y_label("Logical entries, not bytes").y_limits(0,12).size(900,500).save_svg("build/eclat_boundaries.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Exact cap: 28 entries; disabled cap: 28 entries; base/one-short rejections 2/2.
Capture components: raw base 10; root states 10; intersection states 5; emitted ids 3.
Zero threshold on disjoint singletons: Eclat 3; Apriori 3; FP-growth 2; pair support 0.
Sparse ids: 4 rows; 4 raw occurrences; 3 incidences; 2 frequent sets; maximum-id support 2.
Empty input sets 0; empty-basket input sets 0; threshold above N sets 0.
\image(figures/eclat_boundaries.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The tiny run exactly fits 28 entries: 10 raw, 10 singleton-state, five pair-state and three emission entries. A one-entry-short limit fails without returning a partial trace.)
\alttext(A native four-bar plot shows the trace's raw-input, root-state, pair-state and emission costs of ten, ten, five and three logical entries.)

> What the evidence establishes

Tidset intersection gives exact supports without repeated raw-row scans.
The original grocery answers and conditional operands remain visible;
the family checks separate emitted sets, failed attempts and row-merge
work. The Titanic example exercises the canonical dataset and table
encoder using the same core. Plain tidsets can retain substantial memory,
and dense output can be exponential. Actual owned evidence is useful
for inspection, but adds retained history beyond the ordinary result.

> References

- M. J. Zaki (2000), “Scalable Algorithms for Association Mining”, IEEE
  Transactions on Knowledge and Data Engineering 12(3), 372–390,
  [doi:10.1109/69.846291](https://doi.org/10.1109/69.846291).
  Describes the vertical Eclat family; this implementation uses sorted
  tidset lists and prefix extension, without diffsets or bitmap compression.
- [The author's ECLAT repository](https://github.com/zakimjz/ECLAT)
  links the original work and implementation. The datamunge code is its
  existing miner with explicit frames, counters and capture, rather than
  an import of that external implementation.
